# Target C-II — mixed-area competitor for an oblique affine source

**Date:** 2026-09-22  
**Repository commit:** `84d937ca93decb710b829fc610d46e0d89684988`  
**State version:** `2026-09-14.audit.51`  
**Run type:** proof candidate  
**Target:** C-II, restricted affine-lattice source branch  
**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit; noncanonical  

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`.

This note concerns only a finite subset of one exact oblique lattice. It does not
identify a general C-II source with such a lattice and does not settle any global
minimizer quantifier.

## 1. Endpoint and implication chain

The canonical restricted affine theorem `C-OBLIQUE-LATTICE-COMPETITOR` excludes an
oblique lattice source using a radius-2 fundamental-cell bound. Its remaining
obstruction is the inverse-diameter shear window

\[
\sin\theta-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}=O(D^{-1}).
\]

The new bridge is

```text
mixed-area cell bound
  -> exact finite inequality (sin(theta)-sqrt(3)/2) D <= 4 sqrt(3)/pi
     for every surviving affine-lattice global minimizer
  -> stronger exclusion of fixed/mesoscopic affine shear
     (inverse-diameter shear remains open)
  -> restricted C-II frontier only
```

## 2. Strongest new result

### Theorem (mixed-area oblique-lattice competitor)

Let \(u,v\) be unit vectors with
\[
\frac\pi3<\theta:=\angle(u,v)\le\frac\pi2,
\qquad s:=\sin\theta,
\qquad a_0:=\frac{\sqrt3}{2},
\qquad c:=\frac{2\sqrt3}{\pi},
\]
and let \(\Lambda_\theta=\mathbb Zu+\mathbb Zv\). Let
\(A\subset\Lambda_\theta\) be finite, full-dimensional, with
\(n=|A|\ge2\) and \(D=\operatorname{diam}(A)\). If
\[
\boxed{(s-a_0)D>2c=\frac{4\sqrt3}{\pi}},
\tag{MA.1}
\]
then (A) is not a global minimum-diameter (n)-point configuration.

Equivalently, every finite full-dimensional oblique-lattice global minimizer in this
angle range must satisfy the exact necessary bound
\[
\boxed{
\left(\sin\theta-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)D
\le\frac{4\sqrt3}{\pi}.
}
\tag{MA.2}
\]

The reflected statement holds for \(\theta\in[\pi/2,2\pi/3)\) after replacing
\(v\) by \(-v\). At \(\theta=\pi/3\) and \(2\pi/3\), the lattice itself has
unit equilateral triangles and is outside the triangle-free affine branch.

### Proof

First, every finite subset of \(\Lambda_\theta\) is unit-separated in the open
angle range. For a nonzero difference \(iu+jv\), if \(ij\ge0\), then
\[
|iu+jv|^2=i^2+j^2+2ij\cos\theta\ge1.
\]
If \(ij<0\), write \(a=|i|\), \(b=|j|\). Then
\[
|iu+jv|^2=(a-b)^2+2ab(1-\cos\theta)\ge1,
\]
with equality in the open angle range only for the basis differences. Thus no
feasibility is lost in the cell argument.

Let
\[
Q=\{\alpha u+\beta v:0\le\alpha,\beta<1\}
       =[0,u]+[0,v]
\]
be the half-open fundamental cell. Its translates by points of \(A\) have disjoint
interiors and are contained in \(K+Q\), where \(K=\operatorname{conv}A\). Therefore
\[
n s\le \operatorname{area}(K+Q).
\tag{MA.3}
\]

The exact mixed-area estimate is the key improvement. Minkowski additivity for the
two segment summands gives
\[
\operatorname{area}(K+Q)
=\operatorname{area}(K)
 +w_K(u^\perp)+w_K(v^\perp)+\operatorname{area}(Q),
\tag{MA.4}
\]
where \(w_K\) is the width of \(K\). Indeed, adding a segment of unit length in
direction \(u\) increases area by the width perpendicular to \(u\). Since every width
of a set of diameter \(D\) is at most \(D\), and \(\operatorname{area}(Q)=s\),
\[
\operatorname{area}(K+Q)
\le \operatorname{area}(K)+2D+s
\le \frac{\pi D^2}{4}+2D+s.
\tag{MA.5}
\]
Combining (MA.3) and (MA.5),
\[
n s\le \frac{\pi D^2}{4}+2D+s.
\tag{MA.6}
\]

The canonical strict translated-triangular-lattice comparison (Target B Section
36.1--36.2 in `docs/targets/target-b/01-jammed-rhombic-branch.md`) gives, for every
cardinality \(n\),
\[
D(n)^2<c(n-1).
\tag{MA.7}
\]
Subtracting \(s\) in (MA.6) and multiplying by (c/s), using
\(c\pi/4=a_0), yields
\[
c(n-1)
\le \frac{a_0}{s}D^2+\frac{2c}{s}D.
\tag{MA.8}
\]
Set \(\kappa^2=a_0/s<1). Under (MA.1),
\[
\frac{a_0}{s}D^2+\frac{2c}{s}D
<D^2.
\tag{MA.9}
\]
Hence (MA.7) gives \(D(n)<D\), contradicting global minimality of (A).

If (A) is collinear, unit separation gives (n\le D+1), and (MA.7) implies
\(D^2<cD); this is impossible for the large-(D) regime (and can be checked
directly for the finitely many remaining values). Thus the full-dimensional hypothesis
is the only relevant affine case for large sources. The reflected-angle case is the
same argument after changing the sign of one basis vector. \(\square\)

## 3. Comparison with the canonical restricted theorem

The canonical theorem uses (Q\subset B(0,2)), which gives
\[
n s\le \frac{\pi D^2}{4}+2\pi D+4\pi
\]
and the sufficient condition
\((1-\kappa)D>4\kappa+2/\sqrt3\), where
\(\kappa=\sqrt{a_0/s}). The mixed-area proof replaces the isotropic radius-2
boundary charge by the exact two-width charge (2D), and the strict
\(n-1\) triangular comparison cancels the cell-area term (s). The result is the
single exact threshold (MA.1), or (MA.2) for a survivor.

For example, at \(\theta=\pi/2\) the new threshold is
\(D>\frac{4\sqrt3/\pi}{1-\sqrt3/2}\approx16.45\), whereas the old sufficient
threshold is over (70). The numerical comparison is illustrative only; the proof
uses the symbolic inequalities above.

## 4. Scope and hostile audit

- The cell translates need only have disjoint interiors; boundary overlaps do not
  affect (MA.3).
- The estimate is for the actual finite source (A), so contact-isolated points are
  retained.
- No source-to-scaffold identification is made. The theorem does not apply to a
  rhombic grain, a union of affine charts, an interface, a hole, or a macroscopic
  inactive chamber unless an independent affine-lattice hypothesis is supplied.
- The strict comparison (MA.7) is used only as the canonical global triangular-lattice
  competitor; no assertion that the triangular competitor is itself a minimizer is
  needed.
- The threshold still permits \(\sin\theta-a_0=O(D^{-1})\). It therefore does not
  close C-II or Level I, and it does not repair the inverse-diameter shear endpoint.
- The collinear paragraph is auxiliary; no lower-dimensional source is silently used
  in the full-dimensional theorem.

## 5. Exact frontier effect

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit.

The restricted affine-source necessary condition is strengthened from the canonical
bound
\[
\sin\theta-a_0\le\frac{8+4/\sqrt3}{D}
\]
to (MA.2), with constant (4\sqrt3/\pi\approx2.2053). This is a genuine
source-cardinality strict-competitor theorem, but it leaves the inverse-diameter shear
window, general C-II sources, the macroscopic-container branch, and all Level-I
quantifiers unchanged.

## 6. Exact remaining lemma

To use this result for C-II, one still needs a structural theorem placing every
full-core affine-scale survivor in an exact two-direction lattice chart and then a
separate argument eliminating the remaining (O(D^{-1})) shear. No such theorem is
claimed here.
